WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.410
- She's hilarious, she's a great actress,

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:05.210
she's a Golden Globe winner
whom you know from movies

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.780
like "The Farewell" and
"Crazy Rich Asians",

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.410
which little known fact, she
was my favorite character

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.730
in "Crazy Rich Asians".

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:12.690
Hilarious.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.860
Her new movie is called
"Raya and The Last Dragon".

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.870
It's in theaters and on
Disney+ with premier access

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:19.910
on March 5th.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.413
Give it up for Awkwafina, y'all!

00:00:22.616 --> 00:00:26.630
(cheers and applause)
(upbeat music)

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:27.540
Hello!

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.360
- All right, hey!

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.440
- I'm not lying, I have had
another co-star of yours

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.967
from "Crazy Rich Asians" whom I love,

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:37.070
but you are my favorite.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.598
You were so great.

00:00:38.598 --> 00:00:40.537
- Thanks, thank you.

00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:41.370
You're my favorite, dude.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.330
You're awesome.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.080
- Well, you can just say
that and I'll take it.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.910
- No, no, really.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:48.977
- Well, your Comedy Central show is called

00:00:48.977 --> 00:00:51.230
"Awkwafina is Nora From Queens".

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.658
So should I call you Awkwafina or Nora?

00:00:53.658 --> 00:00:56.103
- You can honestly call me
whatever you want, Kelly.

00:00:56.103 --> 00:00:58.394
- Okay, cool.
- Whatever you want.

00:00:58.394 --> 00:00:59.290
- Well now I just wanna make up a name.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:00.890
Now I just wanna call you queen.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.860
- Yes, I'll call you right back.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.638
We could have a mutual-
- I'm totally kidding.

00:01:07.638 --> 00:01:09.620
- I will call you Nora for now,

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:10.870
just because I feel
like everybody probably

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.260
calls you Awkwafina, but I love Awkwafina.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.410
But what did you think of
Anthony and Ranita's story?

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:17.440
Did you think that was so cool?

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.160
I love the never giving up on
your dreams and pursuing it.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.593
- Yeah, I really like what
they said about, you know,

00:01:24.269 --> 00:01:25.910
if you're still breathing, you have time.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.600
I think that it was really beautiful.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.433
I don't know.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.800
I'm not the best guest
to follow up with that

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.660
'cause I don't think that I
was a good student at all,

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.860
but I'm definitely very
inspired by their story.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:39.770
That was awesome.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:40.620
- Yeah.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.796
Even if you're not a good student,

00:01:42.796 --> 00:01:43.629
I just love the fact of like,

00:01:43.629 --> 00:01:45.510
I never thought I'd be a talk show host.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.940
I never had this dream before

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.670
and then your life just
kind of takes changes

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.800
and different journeys that
you didn't really think.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.060
And I love the fact that
like, you don't give up on it,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.930
you just say yes and go,
I don't know, I'll try it.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.090
And I think that that's cool.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.239
'Cause you only get the one life,

00:01:59.239 --> 00:02:01.310
but you went to the Fame
High School in New York.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.210
Did you have to audition to get in there?

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.410
I'm imagining so, right?

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.220
- Yes, yes.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.250
I auditioned, oddly, with the trumpet,

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.823
which I played for a long time.

00:02:13.211 --> 00:02:14.470
And LaGuardia was great,

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:18.200
but you know, it was really
my junior high school years.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.700
I was like, I love the trumpet.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.940
I was like a trumpet hog.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.040
And we would do songs.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.290
My band teacher would bring in songs,

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.300
like we do "Pearl Jam",

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.295
but then we'd also do
like "Livin' la Vida Loca"

00:02:31.295 --> 00:02:34.323
and I remember he actually
pulled me aside and was like,

00:02:35.357 --> 00:02:37.730
"You need to let the woodwinds shine

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.997
because you're taking up too much."

00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:42.900
And it was like, really?

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.590
I'd be like, this is more
of like a brass song,

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:46.423
don't you think?

00:02:46.423 --> 00:02:48.780
I don't think there's a lot of flute solos

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.348
in "Livin' la Vida Loca",
if you know what I mean.

00:02:51.348 --> 00:02:53.369
- I feel like you're wrong, band director.

00:02:53.369 --> 00:02:55.840
(laughter)
- Yeah, I was like,

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.680
excuse you, I'm going to
go and shine as the song

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.849
asks you to do

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:03.652
as a trumpet player.
- Can't stop this, yeah.

00:03:03.652 --> 00:03:05.648
- Yeah.

00:03:05.648 --> 00:03:07.188
- Well, you must've been pretty...

00:03:07.188 --> 00:03:08.310
I always imagine, actually,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.100
one of my first jobs was being
a waitress in a comedy club.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.030
I was a cocktail waitress

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:16.560
and I always imagined like,
'cause they're so hilarious.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.390
Like everybody gets up on stage.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:21.020
Were you like a class
clown or were you shy?

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.260
I always wonder what the
background of that was.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.370
Were you always funny in
class or were you not?

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.240
- I mean, I was definitely
the class clown.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.740
I was definitely the girl

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.310
that didn't really care, you know?

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:36.279
So I also think that, yeah-

00:03:36.279 --> 00:03:38.686
- We would have been friends, by the way,

00:03:38.686 --> 00:03:39.800
like 100%.
- Really, were you

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.250
a class clown?

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.500
- Yeah, I definitely was.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.550
My friends and I, we were those people,

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.909
but definitely I was
the girl that was like,

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:48.285
I don't care about the things

00:03:48.285 --> 00:03:49.405
that you're caring about right now.

00:03:49.405 --> 00:03:51.340
- I've spent time in your hometown,

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.548
I believe, I think.
- You did?

00:03:53.548 --> 00:03:54.947
- Burleson, Texas?

00:03:54.947 --> 00:03:57.165
- Why?
(laughter)

00:03:57.165 --> 00:04:00.400
- Yeah, I'm all about the
Rendon school district,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.240
the Fort Worth, the Mansfield.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.320
- Oh my gosh.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.120
I lived in Mansfield as well.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.057
Fort Worth, Mansfield and Burleson.

00:04:07.057 --> 00:04:08.330
- You lived in Mansfield?

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.163
- Yes!

00:04:09.163 --> 00:04:10.360
- You lived in Mansfield?

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.411
Wow.
- After "American Idol",

00:04:12.411 --> 00:04:13.244
like I had a farm there.

00:04:13.244 --> 00:04:15.840
So you are from Queens, but no,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.930
but Queens is, it's so
interesting for someone

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.110
from a small town in Texas.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.706
So what was it like for
you growing up there?

00:04:21.706 --> 00:04:24.483
- Queens was, I think it was,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.831
you know, it's a very interesting place.

00:04:28.831 --> 00:04:31.230
It's the biggest borough
of all the boroughs,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.770
but there are parts of it
that are pretty suburban,

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.660
but I remember feeling, growing up ,

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.313
that Manhattan, the city was
kind of like a dream, you know?

00:04:41.804 --> 00:04:44.870
And it's funny to be
in a place like Queens,

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.460
'cause you're so close, but
you're so far away from it

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.087
at the same time.

00:04:49.087 --> 00:04:52.670
So it definitely had a small town feel,

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.843
which I'm sure you could
identify with in Mansfield, yes.

00:04:55.843 --> 00:04:58.244
Me and my hydrant, yes.

00:04:58.244 --> 00:05:00.357
(laughter)
- Me and my hydrant.

00:05:00.357 --> 00:05:01.444
- It's not my hydrant.

00:05:01.444 --> 00:05:02.277
- It's mine!

00:05:02.277 --> 00:05:03.880
Did you pee on it, it's yours?

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.581
- I didn't.
(laughter)

00:05:05.581 --> 00:05:06.414
I think a lot of people did pee on it

00:05:06.414 --> 00:05:08.610
right before I grabbed hold of it.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.807
This is pre-COVID times, guys.

00:05:10.807 --> 00:05:13.210
But yes, yes.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.070
- So wait,

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.980
do you look back at what
the people who raised you,

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.600
like what they went through.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.410
Do you look back at what they went through

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:22.330
for you to get your
life and where you are?

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:23.930
'Cause I do that with my mom,

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.510
was like a single mom for a
lot of my life with three kids

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:28.110
putting herself through
college, doing all this stuff.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:32.590
And she really worked so
hard and I now later in life,

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:33.730
I'm just like, oh my gosh.

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:36.610
I look and I thought I had
it hard and like, gosh,

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:38.010
she had it so much harder.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:39.500
Do you often look at
your family like that?

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.120
And then especially
where you're at right now

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.830
being so successful and
really living out your dream.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.080
Do you look at that differently now?

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:46.913
- Yeah.

00:05:46.913 --> 00:05:48.626
I mean, for me,

00:05:48.626 --> 00:05:52.290
I was raised by my
grandma, so I definitely,

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.620
I can relate to what you're talking about,

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.660
that whole single mom journey.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.510
'Cause that's of, you know, I was raised

00:05:58.510 --> 00:05:59.900
by a single dad also.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:03.610
My mom passed when I was four and yeah,

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.080
I mean, it's weird coming back.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.020
I haven't been back to New York City

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.520
in pretty much over a year.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.070
And I think every time
I come back, you know,

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.520
the city changes a little bit more

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.660
and I see my grandma
and she's getting older.

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.250
And I think right now
I'm just really grateful

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:20.270
for where I am and definitely, I think,

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.010
where I came from, you know?

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.410
I think that when you grow up,

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.190
sometimes you just want
to kind of leave town,

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.550
but then you get to a
point where you realize

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:32.120
that just everything that
happened to you is for a reason.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:32.953
You know, so.

00:06:32.953 --> 00:06:34.760
- Absolutely, and those
memories of where you grew up,

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.500
even if it's not where you end up,

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:37.820
it's still such a special place.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.230
Like, regardless because it molded you,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.920
if it was a bad situation
or a good situation,

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.860
it molded you into who you are now.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:44.930
- For sure, yeah.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:47.220
- How do you think tough
times have helped your comedy?

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.040
'Cause like I said, I was
a waitress in a comedy club

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.980
and it was often weird for
me because I figured out

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:55.800
pretty quickly that comedians actually,

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.490
while they're very funny on stage,

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.500
afterwards when they're
waiting for the money

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:03.440
and you're cleaning off your
tables, they go very dark,

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:06.727
a lot of them like, and I was
like, wow, the funny is over.

00:07:06.727 --> 00:07:10.327
So I was always like,
wow, I guess it is true.

00:07:10.327 --> 00:07:13.990
A lot of humor does come
from darkness sometimes

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:16.040
and like tragedy or things like that.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.270
Do you think that that's
helped your comedy at all?

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.407
I mean, I can't imagine,
first of all, losing,

00:07:20.407 --> 00:07:22.010
you know, not having your mother there

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.420
since four, that's horrible.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.640
So like, I can't imagine
even that, like missing it.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:26.730
'Cause I didn't have a father growing up.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.750
So it was like any kind
of daddy daughter thing

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:30.570
or anything that came up
that you kind of wanted

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.230
them to be there for,
that sucks, you know?

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:34.790
And that's gotta affect you a little bit.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:38.226
I think that's often why I
go to humor often as well

00:07:38.226 --> 00:07:40.840
is because I have to
find kind of the funny

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:42.050
in things sometimes.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:43.346
- Yeah.

00:07:43.346 --> 00:07:44.924
I think we're wherever there's light,

00:07:44.924 --> 00:07:46.187
there has to be a darkness.

00:07:46.187 --> 00:07:47.847
I think, you know,

00:07:47.847 --> 00:07:48.810
comedians very often,

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.490
for me, I think it came out because I,

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:52.670
when my mom passed,

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:54.250
I really didn't wanna be like this beacon

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:57.080
of kind of like this sad thing.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.270
I wanted to make people laugh.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.590
I wanted people to be
happy around me, you know?

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:03.068
And so that definitely influenced it.

00:08:03.068 --> 00:08:06.150
I wanted to spread joy and in a weird way,

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:09.070
but I think also, people
would be surprised

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.350
'cause like, yeah, what you said

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.550
about like the comedians
kind of after work.

00:08:15.212 --> 00:08:17.270
There is a kind of a
seriousness that I think

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:18.999
puts people off sometimes,

00:08:18.999 --> 00:08:20.590
because it's just like,
aren't you supposed to be

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:22.370
like super energetic and like crazy?

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:23.630
And it's like, yeah, well, I mean,

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.360
like the other times
I'm just very neurotic

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.540
and worried about everything, you know?

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.220
So there always is, I
think it goes hand in hand.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:32.830
- I think, I feel like
too, not that they have to

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:35.049
like entertain you all
the time, but I feel like

00:08:35.049 --> 00:08:35.882
there's so much energy expended.

00:08:35.882 --> 00:08:38.130
It's like right after I get
off the stage and like tour,

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:39.750
like that night, I don't feel like talking

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:41.140
to a bunch of people.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:41.973
Like, everybody's always
like, "Oh, we're gonna hang."

00:08:41.973 --> 00:08:43.140
And I'm like, "I'm exhausted.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.610
I kind of wanna just chill
and have some downtime."

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:47.840
So I totally, I get it as well.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:48.880
But I have to ask you,

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.910
is it true that when you first started

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:54.910
you pretended to be your
own manager at first?

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:57.385
- Yes, yes I did.

00:08:57.385 --> 00:08:58.830
- How did that go?

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.620
- Not well because his name was Edward.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.243
No last name, didn't
think about it that far.

00:09:04.243 --> 00:09:06.664
So eventually, I realized

00:09:06.664 --> 00:09:10.760
that the male assistant might not work.

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.387
And I had to tell my
friend Maya and I was like,

00:09:13.387 --> 00:09:18.264
"Listen, in case anyone
calls, you work for me, okay?"

00:09:18.264 --> 00:09:19.603
(laughter)
- You work.

00:09:19.603 --> 00:09:20.540
- So it was just like-

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:21.570
- So go along with it.

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:23.741
- Yeah, yeah, pretty much.

00:09:23.741 --> 00:09:26.130
- Wouldn't advise it.
- I love the boldness of it,

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:26.963
of the idea.

00:09:26.963 --> 00:09:28.310
- It was bold.
- Yeah, I totally do this.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:31.240
So I wanted to ask though,

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.490
you said that you were raised
by your dad and your grandma.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:35.360
Are you still pretty
close with your grandma?

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.150
- Yeah, I'm very close.

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:39.270
She's my best friend, she's my therapist.

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:40.541
I talk to her every day.

00:09:40.541 --> 00:09:43.343
Yeah, that was when I
brought her up to "GMA".

00:09:43.343 --> 00:09:44.810
That was awesome.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:45.643
- Aww.

00:09:45.643 --> 00:09:46.700
How does she feel about it?

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.280
You taking her to "GMA"
and how does she feel

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:49.520
about all your success?

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:51.347
- I think she's really happy about it.

00:09:51.347 --> 00:09:54.280
When I was young and I
would always ask her, like,

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:55.790
can I go have singing lessons?

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:57.820
I want to do this.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:58.960
She always supported me,

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.370
even since my first music video.

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:04.574
She was never that grandma that was like,

00:10:04.574 --> 00:10:07.020
"No, go to med school, go to law school."

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:08.840
She always supported my dream.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.030
So very, very thankful for that.

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:14.360
- What an amazing thing to
have that person in your life.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:16.050
Like not everybody gets
that person in your life.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:19.030
That's really cool to have
someone so supportive.

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:20.230
So you said music videos.

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:21.970
So what was your first passion?

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:23.160
Was it comedy, was it music?

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.680
What was it?

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:26.040
- Oh man.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:27.261
I mean passion.

00:10:27.261 --> 00:10:28.170
Well, I think comedy, it came to me

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:30.381
in a really different way.

00:10:30.381 --> 00:10:32.760
Whereas, you know, like music
and making beats and stuff.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:33.593
I mean, I'm sure

00:10:33.593 --> 00:10:36.800
you're like a awesome musician,

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.179
but obviously-

00:10:38.179 --> 00:10:39.680
- I'm not a great musician.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:40.760
- No, you are!
- I'm really good

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:42.530
with this musical instrument.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:46.240
I tried piano and guitar
and I'm so basic at it.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:47.330
It's not a talent.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:49.581
But I can rock the cords.

00:10:49.581 --> 00:10:51.000
- That's what I mean.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.560
You have to learn certain
aspects of music, right?

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.380
So like that's why I think
music is definitely my passion

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:58.110
'cause I love learning more about it.

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:00.577
And I know that I'm not that good at it,

00:11:00.577 --> 00:11:02.675
so I wanna get better and stuff, so yeah.

00:11:02.675 --> 00:11:04.916
- I feel like it's such a release too,

00:11:04.916 --> 00:11:07.700
it's like so therapeutic,
'cause even though I suck,

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:10.150
I still have a piano in
my office, at my house.

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:12.150
And I sit there with my headphones
on so nobody can hear it

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:14.440
and I play it, even if it's bad

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.893
and it makes me feel good.
(laughter)

